	"notes": "The Verge's homepage is a classic two-column news front: one big lead story on the left, and a narrower rail of the latest headlines on the right. What is worth noticing is that the rail is a fixed 380 pixels wide no matter the screen — it does not get any share of a bigger monitor's extra width, because a list of headlines does not get more useful when it is wider. Past 1920px the whole two-column area itself stops growing too and just centres in the middle of the screen. On a phone the rail disappears and the page becomes one long column of story after story. One technical curiosity: a full-width advert sits above the site's own logo and menu on every desktop shot, which is an unusual place to put one.",
